<p>Ask most people what India's biggest spice export markets are, and China rarely comes up first — the Gulf and Southeast Asia dominate the popular image. The trade data says otherwise for dried red chilli specifically: China imported USD 455.8 million of it in calendar 2024, more than three times the next-largest market (Thailand, at USD 136.7 million), out of USD 1.3 billion in total Indian chilli exports worldwide. Two things shape every China-bound order: the variety and colour/heat specification you buy against, and GACC facility registration under Decree 248, which must be in place before a first shipment can clear customs. This guide covers both, plus pricing, container loading and the first-order pitfalls.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>Which chilli variety should you order for the Chinese market: Guntur, Byadgi or Teja?</h2>
<p>"Red chilli" is not a usable order specification — India exports at least three genuinely different products under that heading:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Guntur (Sannam / S4)</strong> — the volume workhorse, balanced colour and moderate-to-high heat.</li>
  <li><strong>Byadgi</strong> — deep red colour with comparatively low pungency, used where colour is the objective (including oleoresin extraction).</li>
  <li><strong>Teja</strong> — very high pungency, used where heat is the primary objective.</li>
</ul>
<p>The variety follows from what the chilli ends up in. Heat comes from capsaicin, and SHU is a measure of how much of it the pod carries. Colour comes from separate pigments, measured as ASTA colour. A buyer can therefore pay for one without the other:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hot-pot bases and chilli oil:</strong> heat and red colour both show in the bowl, so Guntur S4 or a Guntur and Teja blend is the usual starting point.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour extraction and oleoresin:</strong> the processor pays for pigment, not pungency. Byadgi's high ASTA colour with low heat gives more colour per tonne of raw material and less capsaicin to manage downstream.</li>
  <li><strong>Hot sauce and chilli powder blends:</strong> Teja supplies the heat, so a smaller share of it in a blend reaches a target SHU, and a Byadgi or Guntur share brings the colour back up.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scenario: a Chinese seasoning maker blending its own powder buys Teja for heat and Byadgi for colour in separate lots, then blends to its own recipe. That costs less than buying a pre-blended powder and lets it adjust the ratio by season. Tell us which application you're buying for and we'll recommend a variety rather than quoting against an ambiguous request.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>How is chilli colour and heat specified on a contract?</h2>
<p>Colour is measured in ASTA colour value; heat in Scoville Heat Units (SHU) or ASTA pungency. Beyond that, moisture and admixture percentages determine grade. Give us target values for the parameters that matter to your application and we'll quote precisely rather than against a vague "export quality" tier.</p>
<p>For China, add two lines beyond colour and heat: pesticide residues tested against GB 2763, China's national standard for pesticide maximum residue limits, and stemless or with-stem stated explicitly, because stems count as weight you pay for.</p>

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Who buys Indian chilli in China, and why does China pay less per kilo than the US?</h2>
<p>China buys for volume, and pays close to the world average for it. Dividing the WITS 2024 values by tonnage gives these average unit values for Indian dried chilli:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Market (2024)</th><th scope="col">Tonnes</th><th scope="col">Average unit value (USD/kg)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>China</td><td>186,559</td><td>2.44</td></tr>
<tr><td>World (all markets)</td><td>537,599</td><td>2.41</td></tr>
<tr><td>United States</td><td>38,850</td><td>3.40</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>5,148</td><td>4.14</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sri Lanka</td><td>42,771</td><td>1.84</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>China took 34.7% of India's chilli by weight and 35.1% by value, so its average price sits almost exactly on the world figure. The US paid about 39% more per kilo than China. Read that gap as a difference in what is bought rather than a better deal. The trade data does not split by form or grade, but a buyer paying more per kilo is usually paying for more processing, tighter sorting or a narrower residue spec. A large share of Chinese demand is whole pods bought by processors (hot-pot base makers, seasoning manufacturers, colour extractors) and by traders reselling into the wholesale markets, and whole pods carry less processing cost.</p>
<p>What this means for a buyer in China: you are competing for the same Guntur and Teja lots as Thailand, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka, at similar unit values, so specification and timing (see price, below) matter more than negotiating a discount. Source: unit values calculated from WITS/UN Comtrade HS 090420, India exports by partner, 2024.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How many tonnes of chilli pods fit in a 20ft container?</h2>
<p>About 12-14 MT of bagged whole pods in a 20ft and 22-24 MT in a 40ft. Dried pods are light and bulky, so chilli fills the box by volume long before it reaches the weight limit. That is the opposite of rice or pulses, and it changes the container choice.</p>
<p>At the midpoints, a 40ft carries 23 MT against 13 MT in a 20ft: 1.77 times the tonnage. The 40ft is cheaper per tonne whenever its freight rate is less than 1.77 times the 20ft rate on your lane, which is a much easier test to pass than for dense cargo. A 20ft of whole pods at China's 2024 average unit value holds roughly USD 32,000 of chilli, so the freight on a half-empty-by-weight box is a larger share of landed cost than buyers used to grain expect.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Qingdao importer planning 50 MT a quarter can book four 20ft containers (about 52 MT) or two 40ft (about 46 MT) plus a top-up. Compare the two rates, divided by tonnage, before deciding. Our default MOQ is one 20ft container, so a first trial lot of a new variety can still move in the smaller box. Packing in 25kg rather than 50kg bags does not change the tonnage much, but it makes hand-loading tighter and the stack more stable.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:policy-timeline -->
<p>China requires facility-level registration with the General Administration of Customs of China — administered through the CIFER online system — before many food categories, chilli included, can clear customs. This is a process with real lead time, tied to the specific processing facility rather than the trading relationship alone. If you're working with a new supplier for China-bound chilli, confirming CIFER registration status is the first practical question to ask, well before discussing price or specification in detail.</p>
<p>The rule behind it changed this year. Decree 248 applied from 1 January 2022. GACC Decree 280 replaced it on 1 June 2026, according to a Singapore Food Agency trade circular on the change. Registrations now last five years, renewal can be filed between 3 and 12 months before expiry, and categories such as seasoning powder, dehydrated vegetables and dried fruits must be recommended by the exporting country's competent authority rather than self-registered. Existing registrations stay valid, but their expiry dates still matter. Scenario: a facility registered in early 2022 under Decree 248 reaches its five-year expiry in early 2027, so its renewal window is already open. A contract for spring 2027 delivery should name who confirms the renewal, and by when.</p>
<p>At the port, the goods are inspected by GACC's customs inspection teams, still widely called CIQ (China Inspection and Quarantine) by importers, although CIQ was merged into customs in 2018.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
<p>China maintains its own residue and quality standards, not harmonised with EU or US limits. Product cleared for one market isn't automatically cleared for China. Flag China as your destination from the start of the conversation and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese requirement.</p>
<p>Spices attract close regulatory attention across major markets, China included. We arrange pre-shipment testing on request and can provide traceability documentation linking a shipment back to its sourcing lot — useful if a quality question arises after delivery. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>

