<!-- insight:rejection-patterns -->
<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common technical dispute on chilli shipments is an ASTA colour-value mismatch between buyer and supplier labs. ASTA colour is genuinely sensitive to sample handling — how the sample was ground for testing, how long it sat before measurement, and even ambient humidity during preparation can shift the reading by a meaningful margin. Ask what sample-preparation method our certificate of analysis uses, and if you're running your own lab check on arrival, prepare the sample the same way rather than assuming any lab's number is automatically comparable to any other.</p>
<p>The second real issue is moisture and aflatoxin risk. Dried chilli is a genuine aflatoxin-risk commodity — pods that weren't fully dried before storage, or that pick up moisture during a humid transit, create conditions for mould growth and aflatoxin development that can fail a shipment on arrival testing even if it looked fine on departure. We dry to spec and pack in moisture-appropriate packaging specifically to manage this, and we'd recommend asking any supplier directly about their drying and storage practice rather than assuming it's standard — it isn't always.</p>
<p>Third, GACC CIFER registration timing catches first-time China buyers more than any other single issue on this product. Registration is tied to the specific exporting facility and needs to be confirmed — or initiated, if it isn't already in place — well before you commit to a delivery date with your own downstream customer. We recommend confirming registration status as the very first question in a new China-bound relationship, not after specification and price are already agreed.</p>
<p>What first-time China-bound buyers most often get wrong: ordering "red chilli" without specifying variety, then being surprised that the colour or heat level doesn't match what they expected. Guntur, Byadgi and Teja are commercially distinct products that happen to share a plant family — treat the variety choice as the first specification decision, not an afterthought to price.</p>
<p>Fourth, Sudan dye. Sudan I-IV are synthetic industrial dyes that have been found illegally added to chilli products to boost colour, and they are banned in food. A lot whose ASTA colour looks too good for its variety is the warning sign. The clause that prevents a dispute: a certificate of analysis that reports Sudan I-IV as not detected, from a named lab, on the lot being shipped, together with aflatoxin tested against the Chinese limit and moisture at or below the contract's 10-12%, since damp pods are where aflatoxin starts. If the arrival test disagrees, the contract should already name the referee lab.</p>
